{"qid":"Q43","names":["Republic of Turkey"],"title":"Republic of Turkey","endonym":{"native":"Türkiye","lang":"tr"},"capsule":"The Republic of Turkey was proclaimed on 29 October 1923 by Mustafa Kemal Atatürk after the War of Independence (1919–23) expelled Greek, French, and Italian forces and secured the boundaries established by the Treaty of Lausanne (July 1923), which also imposed a population exchange of approximately 1.3 million Greeks and 400,000 Muslims. Atatürk's government then enacted sweeping secularizing reforms: abolition of the caliphate (1924), replacement of Islamic law with European legal codes, the Latin alphabet (1928), and the surname law (1934). Turkey remained neutral in World War II, joined NATO in 1952, and has pursued EU membership since the 1980s while navigating recurrent tensions between secularist and Islamist political currents through the present.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Turkey's international borders are precisely established through the 1923 Treaty of Lausanne and subsequent bilateral agreements; there are minor unresolved issues in the Aegean and Cyprus question that do not affect mainland mapping, making the territorial extent highly confident for the 1924–2024 period.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Turkey","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Turkey"},{"label":"Britannica: Turkey — History","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Turkey/History"}],"britannica":"place/Turkey","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Turkey","rulers":[{"qid":"Q5152","name":"Mustafa Kemal Atatürk","nativeName":"Mustafa Kemal Atatürk","roman":"Mustafa Kemal Ataturk","sta